      "text": "EAT IRON-RICH FOODS\n          •   Plant foods like green leafy vegetables, pulses and dry fruits contain iron.\n          •   Iron is also obtained through meat, fish, eggs and poultry products.\n          •   Vitamin C rich fruits like guava, pineapple and citrus fruits or chutneys made\n              with coriander and green chillies improve iron absorption from plant foods,\n              when consumed as part of a regular meal.\n          •   Beverages like tea bind dietary iron and make it unavailable. Hence, they\n              should be avoided for at least an hour before, during or soon after a meal.\n\n\n\n\n    EAT CALCIUM-RICH FOODS\n•   Children require higher level of                         Children should consume atleast\n    calcium for growth and bone                              200–400 g vegetables depending\n    development.                                             on age (refer Table 1.6).\n•   Milk, curd, sesame seeds, ragi and\n    GLVs like amaranth are good\n    sources of calcium.\n•   Regular exercise reduces calcium\n    loss and strengthens bones.\n•   Exposure to sunlight (about 30                                    DURING ILLNESS\n    minutes preferably between 11.00                •   Feed energy-giving nutrient-rich cereals-pulse\n    am to 2.00 pm) maintains vitamin                    diet with milk and mashed vegetables and fruits\n    D status which helps in calcium                     during recovery and post illness.\n    absorption.\n                                                    •   Feed small quantities at frequent intervals.\n                                                    •   Give plenty of water and other fluids.\n\n\n\n                                                   42"
